import argparse
import json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
parser=argparse.ArgumentParser(description='Render the fixed T03 report template from validated T03 aggregate tables.')
parser.add_argument('--assessment-dir',type=Path,default=R/'results/features/behavioral_v1')
parser.add_argument('--output-dir',type=Path,default=R/'docs/trung')
parser.add_argument('--overwrite',action='store_true',help='Allow replacing the three existing T03 reports')
args=parser.parse_args()
O=args.assessment_dir.resolve(); D=args.output_dir.resolve()
if D == R or D == R/'data' or (R/'data') in D.parents or D == O:
 raise ValueError('Report destination must not be repository root, inside data/, or the assessment directory')
report_names=['S1_T03_FEATURE_QUALITY_REPORT.md','S1_T03_INTEGRATION_READINESS.md','S1_T03_EXECUTION_LOG.md']
if not args.overwrite and any((D/name).exists() for name in report_names):
 raise FileExistsError('Reports exist; select an empty --output-dir or explicitly use --overwrite')
import pandas as pd
j=json.loads((O/'assessment_summary.json').read_text()); s=pd.read_csv(O/'feature_statistics.csv'); n=pd.read_csv(O/'novelty_rates.csv'); fr=pd.read_csv(O/'train_fraud_comparison.csv');
def table(df):
 df=df.copy()
 for c in df.columns:
  if pd.api.types.is_float_dtype(df[c]): df[c]=df[c].map(lambda x: 'NA' if pd.isna(x) else f'{x:.4g}')
 return '| '+' | '.join(df.columns)+' |\n|'+'|'.join(['---']*len(df.columns))+'|\n'+'\n'.join('| '+' | '.join(map(str,row))+' |' for row in df.itertuples(index=False,name=None))
# This report contains fixed T03 narrative and is valid only for the original reviewed artifact set.
expected={'train':'5184322dc4b88f73fa59a091406d34b746c253c93533f48427c2589c818d32b1','validation':'cb34d6e44f194eb927c460e4da78a349536a64b48a93271182093a69e12c8b63','test':'7bd3d66ac276e0837d94ad2596b9beb450606a2b809b1ed45d13c13c150e3093'}
if any(j.get('hashes',{}).get(k)!=v for k,v in expected.items()) or not all(j.get('checks',{}).values()) or not j.get('invariants_pass'):
 raise ValueError('Report template only supports the original passing T03 artifact set; review results before reporting')
if len(s)!=30 or set(s['split'])!={'train','validation','test'} or len(n)!=6 or len(fr)!=20:
 raise ValueError('Unexpected aggregate table shapes; cannot render the fixed T03 narrative')
D.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
cols=['split','feature','mean','median','p95','p99','missing_rate','zero_rate_all_rows']; summary=table(s[cols]); fraud=table(fr[['label','feature','rows','mean','median']]); nv=table(n)
quality='''# S1-T03 — Feature Quality Assessment\n\n## Executive summary\n\n**COMPLETE, conditional handoff.** Direct inspection of all 1,852,394 rows shows artifact integrity, schema, one-to-one manifest coverage and the requested behavioral invariants pass. Ten feature definitions remain unchanged. Git status remains unavailable in this environment, so repository handoff is conditional. This is descriptive analysis, not model performance or feature selection.\n\n## Integrity\n\nAll four files exist; SHA-256 of each Parquet matches metadata, processed/manifest input checksums match metadata, feature version and order match T01, and split hash matches config, metadata, and independently calculated manifest. Three Arrow count fields are int32, seven other features float64, ID Arrow string; no target/card/source/split predictor occurs. IDs are non-null/unique and exactly cover each manifest split, counts train 1,037,340, validation 259,335, test 555,719. No source or feature artifact was written by assessment. Full hashes and run details are in the execution log.\n\n## Statistics and distribution\n\nComplete per-feature/per-split statistics including valid/missing counts and rates, infinities, min/max, mean, median, sample standard deviation (ddof=1), P01/P05/P25/P75/P95/P99, zero fraction of all rows and unique non-null counts are in `results/features/behavioral_v1/feature_statistics.csv`. Zero rate uses all rows as denominator, with missing included in denominator. The table below shows summary values; units remain as specified by T01.\n\n'''+summary+'''\n\nNovelty rates (denominator all rows):\n\n'''+nv+'''\n\nMerchant novelty falls from 42.64% train to 14.16% validation and 9.04% test, consistent with growing observed history as a possible contributor; no causal attribution is established. Median 7-day counts are 23, 23 and 27; median ratio is 0.666, 0.675 and 0.669. Train-derived P99 clipping is used only for comparable chart display, not to change data or fit preprocessing. These are descriptive time-split differences; no arbitrary drift threshold, PSI, or test-driven selection is used.\n\n## Missing and behavioral plausibility\n\nAll 999 cards have one earliest event: 999 cold-start rows. Each of mean, std, ratio, recency and hour deviation has exactly 999 missing values, all on those earliest rows; zero missing in non-cold rows. This directly supports cold-start as their observed cause in this dataset; it does not establish that other undefined cases cannot occur in future data. Production merchant/category inputs have no nulls, novelty outputs are 0/1 without missing. Amounts are positive. There are zero infinities, negative counts/amount mean/std/ratio, nonpositive defined recencies, out-of-range hours, or count-order violations. Counts are int32. There are zero cold-start violations for count, history, recency or novelty.\n\nAll **44** tied card/timestamp groups (88 rows) were checked: counts, historical mean/std, recency and hour deviation have the same pre-group values, including matching NaN; zero violating groups. Ratio and novelty can depend on the current transaction and were not required to be equal. Existing T02 synthetic tests also address future injection, ties, card isolation and label independence; this data inspection does not establish online availability or parity.\n\n## Correlation — train only\n\nPearson and Spearman use pairwise complete observations; NaNs are not imputed. Full 10×10 matrices are `train_pearson.csv` and `train_spearman.csv`. There are no pairs with |r| >= 0.9 in either matrix (this is a descriptive flag, not a selection rule). Pearson/Spearman respectively: 1h vs 24h 0.342/0.308; 24h vs 7d 0.703/0.686; historical mean vs std 0.313/0.372; historical mean vs ratio -0.032/-0.001; std vs ratio -0.034/-0.107; merchant vs category novelty 0.131/0.131. No feature is removed.\n\n## Fraud relevance — train labels only\n\nTrain has 1,031,372 normal and 5,968 fraud rows; feature calculation did not access labels. Detailed descriptive comparisons are in `train_fraud_comparison.csv`. The table shows means and medians; differences may be affected by skew, time and limited fraud sample. No validation/test labels were read for this section.\n\n'''+fraud+'''\n\nFor example, median amount ratio is 0.662 for normal and 5.164 for fraud; merchant novelty fraction is 42.56% vs 57.29%. These are observational associations on train and do not prove improvements in PR-AUC or recall. Small fraud subgroups, including novel category fraud events, warrant caution; no threshold is fitted.\n\n## Limits\n\nSparkov is simulated, timestamp timezone and amount currency are unspecified. Cross-split history assumes preceding non-target events are observable at scoring time. Review Git/LFS and unreviewed Toàn fixture before handoff. Output visuals: five PNGs in `results/features/behavioral_v1/`; `distribution_comparison.png` clips at train P99 for display.\n'''
(D/'S1_T03_FEATURE_QUALITY_REPORT.md').write_text(quality,encoding='utf-8')
(D/'S1_T03_INTEGRATION_READINESS.md').write_text('''# S1-T03 — Integration readiness\n\n**Verdict: CONDITIONAL GO.** Ten fixed feature names and order, exact Arrow types, checksums, split hash and ID coverage pass on all 1,852,394 rows. The approved output is `data/features/behavioral_v1/{train,validation,test}.parquet`, one row per `trans_num`, with metadata in the same directory. Canonical source is `data/processed/transactions.parquet`, split assignment `data/splits/split_manifest.parquet`.\n\nTú/Tùng: load matching split, validate checksum and split hash, join transaction labels and features by `trans_num` with one-to-one validation and equal coverage. Exclude ID, card, target, source metadata and split from X. Undefined history remains NaN; fit imputation/encoding/scaling only on train. Tùng samples or weights training only. Khoa keys scores with `trans_num`, records split, model, feature version and split hash. See `S1_T02_HANDOFF_GUIDE.md`.\n\nStrict past-only and same-timestamp isolation pass artifact checks plus existing T02 synthetic leakage tests; no labels are used by feature generation. The observed-history policy assumes earlier validation/test non-target transactions are available when later events are scored; online replay, watermark and late-event behavior need operational validation. Source timezone/currency remain unspecified.\n\n**Outstanding:** Git status and ignore resolution failed with `fatal: this operation must be run in a work tree`; `.gitignore` does not explicitly ignore `data/features`. Check before staging, review Toàn's existing test change and generated EDA/manifest, and resolve LFS status. No test split labels were used for feature selection or model training.\n''',encoding='utf-8')
hashes='\n'.join(f'- {k}: `{v}`' for k,v in j['hashes'].items())
(D/'S1_T03_EXECUTION_LOG.md').write_text(f'''# S1-T03 — Execution log\n\nBranch `trung/feature-engineering-s1`; HEAD `0161186bdc1ac0eb0d88a7638dad67d1b9fbf029`. `git status --short --branch --untracked-files=all` returned `fatal: this operation must be run in a work tree`; no clean-tree assertion is made.\n\nPython {j['python']}; pandas {j['pandas']}; PyArrow {j['pyarrow']}. Command from repo root: `.\\.venv-clean\\Scripts\\python.exe docs/trung/t03_assess.py`. Script uses only read access to input files and writes assessment outputs. Runtime {j['runtime_seconds']} s; peak Windows working set {j['peak_working_set_bytes']:,} bytes during assessment, not feature generation.\n\nInput paths: `data/processed/transactions.parquet`, `data/splits/split_manifest.parquet`, split config/metadata; features `data/features/behavioral_v1/`. Split hash `{j['split_hash']}`. SHA-256:\n\n{hashes}\n\nOutputs: `results/features/behavioral_v1/` contains `feature_statistics.csv`, `novelty_rates.csv`, `train_pearson.csv`, `train_spearman.csv`, `train_fraud_comparison.csv`, `assessment_summary.json` and five PNG charts. Reports are the three S1_T03 Markdown documents in `docs/trung/`.\n\nWarnings/limits: Git status unavailable; input data timezone and currency unknown. Display plots clip continuous distributions at train P99. Correlations use pairwise complete train rows; no imputation. Fraud relevance joins only the pre-existing train labels. Files contain aggregate statistics; no feature regeneration or upstream overwrite occurred.\n''',encoding='utf-8')
